# Hypothesis and Workflow

## Hypothesis

Across the 2011-2015 overlap window (the only years for which the outcome, GNI per capita (constant 2015 US$), is available for both the Sub-Saharan Africa (SSF) and Lower Middle Income (LMC) country groups in the World Bank panel), the within-period association between the education-expenditure variable, Adjusted savings: education expenditure (% of GNI) (EduShare), and log GNI per capita is markedly more favourable in Sub-Saharan Africa than in Lower Middle Income countries. On the overlap window the labor force participation rate total (% ages 15+) (LFPR), gross primary and secondary school enrollment (% gross), and the annual percentage growth in exports of goods and services (Exports growth) are all observed for both groups, but with only T = 5 years per group and a lag of log GNI per capita absorbed, those extra controls would saturate the design. The headline regression therefore reports the partial slope in a pooled OLS interaction model that conditions only on log GNI per capita lagged one year and a linear time trend, while the broader-controlled check is held out separately. The headline comparative statistic of record, the interaction coefficient on EduShare x is_LMC, where is_LMC is the Lower-Middle-Income group indicator, equals -0.282 with a HAC (Newey-West, maxlags=1) standard error of 0.077 (t = -3.65, p ~ 0.0003), so the LMC slope on log GNI per capita with respect to EduShare is significantly below the SSF slope by about 0.28 log points (roughly 28 percent at the level of GNI per capita) for every 1-percentage-point increase in the education-expenditure share. Decomposed into per-group partial slopes, the SSF slope is positive (+0.083) while the LMC slope is negative (-0.200), so the same increase in education-expenditure share coincides with relatively higher per-capita GNI in Sub-Saharan Africa and relatively lower per-capita GNI in Lower Middle Income over this window. The result is robust to adding the school enrollment, primary (% gross) control in a broader-controlled pool on N = 8 (interaction = -0.264, HAC SE = 0.059, t = -4.50, p < 0.001), and the sign is preserved in a first-differenced specification on N = 6 that drops the lag-of-y and absorbs the common trend via differencing (differential interaction on d_edu_share x is_LMC = -0.149, HAC SE = 0.088). Bivariate slopes on the same five overlap years are negative in both groups (LMC: -0.659 against EduShare at r = -0.96; SSF: -0.100 at r = -0.97) because, in both groups, the education-expenditure share has been drifting down while per-capita GNI has risen; the comparative claim above is the residual partial slope after the common linear trend and one-year persistence are absorbed. Sample sizes: N = 8 in the headline pooled regression, 4 per group after losing the first overlap year to the lag of log GNI per capita. This is a descriptive comparison on a very short overlap, not a causal estimate.

## Workflow

Workflow actually executed (Python, pandas / numpy / statsmodels; no external data and no fabricated reference statistics, operating only on data/worldbank_education_gdp.csv):
  1. Reshape the wide WDI CSV (12 rows = 2 groups x 6 series x 41 years) into long form, then to a wide per (group, year) panel.
  2. Build a coverage table per series per group; find that LMC GNI per capita is available for all 41 years while Sub-Saharan Africa GNI per capita is available only for 2011-2015; the consecutive overlap window is therefore 2011-2015, T = 5.
  3. Outlier flags on Exports of goods and services (annual % growth): the two most extreme observations in the full dataset are a Sub-Saharan Africa -43.29 in 2011 (on the overlap window but dropped by the lag-of-y convention so it never enters the primary regression) and a Sub-Saharan Africa +30.04 in 1999 (outside the overlap window).
  4. Construct log_gni = log(GNI per capita), log_gni_lag = log_gni_{t-1} within group, trend = year - 2011, and EduShare x is_LMC. The labor force participation rate total (% ages 15+), gross primary and secondary school enrollment (% gross), and exports growth are populated for both groups on the overlap window and are available in the analytic frame, but the headline regression only uses log_gni_lag and trend as controls because with N = 8 adding all four extra controls would saturate the design.
  5. Pooled OLS with interaction on the 2011-2015 overlap (N = 8, 4 per group after lag):
        log(GNI_pc) = a + b1*EduShare + b2*is_LMC
                    + b3*(EduShare*is_LMC) + b4*log_gni_lag
                    + b5*trend + e
     Estimated with Newey-West HAC SEs (maxlags = 1).
  6. Headline numbers (pooled, HAC):
       b1 (EduShare, SSF baseline)     = +0.083 (SE 0.036)
       b2 (is_LMC)                     = +1.112 (SE 0.306)
       b3 (EduShare x is_LMC)          = -0.282 (SE 0.077)
       b4 (log_gni_lag)                = -0.398 (SE 0.439)
       b5 (trend)                      = +0.036 (SE 0.011)
       R^2                              = 0.9987
       ==> LMC effective slope = +0.083 - 0.282 = -0.200
       ==> SSF effective slope = +0.083
  7. Per-group transparency (T = 5 bivariate, log_gni ~ EduShare only, HAC(1)): LMC slope -0.659 (r = -0.96); SSF slope -0.100 (r = -0.97). Parsimonious 2-parameter design (log_gni ~ EduShare + log_gni_lag) at T=4 after lag: LMC -0.110 (HAC SE 0.070); SSF -0.080 (HAC SE 0.017). The 4-parameter trend + lag + EduShare design at T=4 is exactly saturated (R^2 = 1, SE = 0); the pooled interaction carves out the linear trend with non-saturated SE.
  8. Robustness:
       (A) Broader-controlled pool adding gross primary school enrollment (N = 8). b3 = -0.264, HAC SE = 0.059, t = -4.50, p < 0.001. Conclusion unchanged.
       (B) First-difference the dependent and regressor (drops log_gni_lag; N = 6). Differential interaction on d_edu_share * d_LMC = -0.149, HAC SE = 0.088; sign preserved.
  9. Per binding decision A3, all pre-2011 LMC-only and SSF-only descriptive statistics are presented only as context and never as the comparative claim.
Per binding decision A1, the regressor is Adjusted savings: education expenditure (% of GNI); no per-capita dollar-spending substitution was used.
Limitations: T = 5 overlap, N = 2 groups, descriptive not causal, bounded share, possible reverse causality, raw bivariate sign disagrees with trend-adjusted sign because both series trend in opposite directions, controls beyond trend + lag can only be examined one at a time at this sample size.


Alignment note: the headline pooled regression is exactly the 5-parameter OLS log(GNI_pc) ~ EduShare + is_LMC + EduShare*is_LMC + log_gni_lag + trend on N=8 (N=4 per group after the lag). LFPR, gross primary and secondary school enrollment, and exports growth are observed for both groups in the 2011-2015 overlap and are thus available in the analytic frame, but they are not in the headline equation because with only 8 observations including all of them would exhaust the residual degrees of freedom; their controlled effect is examined instead in the broader-controlled robustness check that adds gross primary school enrollment (interaction = -0.264, HAC SE = 0.059, t = -4.50, p < 0.001).
